Date: Wed, 27 Jan 93 10:43:38 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Futures vs. Equities

Perry and I seem to being going in circles.

Perry: "futures are a zero sum game, unlike, say, the equities market"

Robin: "Can you offer a more precise definition of "zero-sum" so that 
        we could evaluate your claim?"

Perry: "On the other hand, the futures market is a betting market in which
        whenever I make money it is because someone else has lost it."

Robin: "If you (Perry) say that futures is "zero-sum", show me a set
        of relevant numbers that add up to zero."

Perry continues to tell stories about how the price of one stock can
rise without the price of others falling, and about how when folks bet
the sum of their assets stays the same.  I continue to point out that
this all depends on the unit of account one uses to measure prices and
assets.  I have explicitly, by construction, shown units of account
wherein over time a sum of stock prices stays constant (relative to
S&P500), and where a sum of betting assets increases (relative to $).

To which Perry responds: 
       "A "zero sum" game is a game in which there are a fixed number
        of points, chips, dollars, or whatever, at the start of the
        game, and in which at the end only the allocation of the
        points has changed."

But this definition doesn't distinguish futures from equities if it
doesn't specify how to measure "points"; "or whatever" doesn't cut it.
It isn't even useful in game theory, as game strategy is unchanged if
we add a constant number of points to each player's payoff in all
possible outcomes.

Uncle!  I conclude this conversation has failed.  Sorry to waste
everyone else's time.

Robin Hanson

