Date: Wed, 27 Jan 93 10:48:21 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ANARCHY: The Twilight of Intellectual Property?

My long essay on this subject was enough for now, so I'll keep this
response very brief.

Tom Morrow writes:

> I note that Tim May's recent post about the impact of technology on 
> intellectual property rights appears to apply only to copyrights.

Mostly to "software" rather than "hardware," yes. Hardware can still
be protected, as I alluded to with the Light Signatures example.
Software, as music, video, ideas, programming, etc., is nearly
impossible to protect.

> Granted, his comments about medieval guilds could be said to regard the
> impact of the free exchange of information on what was once analogous to
> patents.  I think a better analogy would be to trade secrets, however.  And
> in any case Tim does not seem to be saying that *current* technological
> developments are putting patents at risk.  Instead, he appears to take aim
> only at copyrights.

Trade secrets should be just that--secrets. 

Consider this case (which ought to disturb a few people): encryption
of satellite video signals. Is breaking the encryption theft? In what
way?

Do you remember the case of the guy who was hauled into court and
charged with breaking the VideoCypher I system? He argued, as I
recall, that he didn't _ask_ for radio signals to be sent into his
yard, and all he was doing was monitoring them to see if they were
dangerous! (I think the court dismissed the case, but I could be
wrong.)

A simple example:

[Tthhiiss mmeessaaggee iiss eennccrryypptteedd..
Uunnaauutthhoorriizzeedd rreeaaddiinngg ooff iitt iiss aa
ccrriimmee..]

The point is this: if I post or broadcast a message and someone else
is able to read it--or whatever they want to do with it--can I claim
they committed a crime? Forget the abstract issues of morality (Perry
and David Friedman can rejoice)...what PPL system in the world would
hunt down and prosecute someone who "illegally" read a posted message?

The solution, to the extent there _is_ one, is better encryption. (The
satellite business is facing this battle. Tamper-resistant decoders
tie the decryption to a piece of hardware. Eventually this will fail.)

Reputations still matter. If I know, for example, that Harry Shapiro
has "defected" by buying bootleg software, I can avoid selling him my
latest product. (He may still get it, eventually, but "slowing him
down" may be enough, or at least the best that can be hoped for.)

Let me note the PPL aspects. I don't want to _pay_ for the many
detectives and cops it will take to try to enforce the video
encryption laws. Better that General Instruments and HBO pay the costs
for protecting (if they can) their own property. Nor do I want to pay
for the prisons to put "video pirates" in. Etc. Sounds like PPL to me.

> Tim's comments are clearly directed at copyrights (though you could argue
> that software copying threatens what few software patents there are).  But
> while Tim's comments about the erosion of copyrights deserves our attention
> and critical scrutiny, they do not appear to aim at *all* of intellectual
> property.  What about other forms of intellectual property?  What about
> patents, trademarks, and trade secrets?

Patents are increasingly software, anyway. The examples of "widgets"
that are used, like paperclips and ball-point pens, are too
simplistic. Modern patents are for things like the XOR cursor (yes,
somebody got a patent for the obvious, and long-used, idea of XORing
the cursor with whatever's underneath it, so it's always visible. This
company is now going around using the power of the state to threaten
other companies into paying royalties).

Trade secrets, as I said, are just that. Was the tanning of leather a
trade secret? You bet. And once it was out, it was not. Modern trade
secret laws are the equivalent of guilds.

Trademarks are best enforced by reputation mechnisms and independent
testing agencies. 

> The increasing ease with which information can be transferred may adversely
> impact trade secrets, but only insofar as it allows people to violate their
> contractual agreements not to disclose confidential information.  Computer
> technology can likewise aid theives and murderers who need to conspire in
> private.  I leave working out the moral implications as an exercise to the
> reader.

When employees can log into "Black AMIX" through a series of cutouts
nad anonymoumous forwarding systems and offer corporate secrets for
sale, all hell will break loose.

(It was precisely this scenario that I worked out in December of 1987
as Phil Salin, the founder of AMIX (the American Information
Exchange), explained his plan to me. I noted to him, and he
reluctantly agreed, that the most "valuable" knowledge most people
have is, currently, related to their jobs. If a chip company offers,
through anonymous systems, $20K for the answer to a question about ion
implantation through metal layers, someone at Intel may very well
decide to collect an extra $20K, all untraceably. This raises lots of
issues, which I won't go into here.)

Enough for now.

-Tim May


