Dale Worley wrote:

    ". . . a new PPL, called "Minerva" was founded a few
    years ago when some people dumped a load of fill on a
    shallow underwater seamount.  Unfortunately, it was
    conquored by the PPL of Fiji, and since it had not
    arranged a mutual-support pact with any other PPL, it
    had no effective defense against attack."

Actually, the reason that Minerva fell to Tonga (not Fiji) was
due to the non-violent beliefs of Mike Oliver, the founder of
Minerva.  I spoke with Mr. Oliver 15 years, or so, ago.  He had
emigrated from Eastern Europe where he had seen too much violence
and death.  He didn't believe in using force to defend the new
country he had literally dredged out of the sea.  He believed
(naively, in my opinion) Minerva's sovereignty would be readily
recognized by the international community.

One day, when most or all of the workers were off the atoll,
Tongans landed there and claimed it for the Kingdom of Tonga.
(The story--probably apocryphal--was that the King of Tonga got
some "volunteers" from jail and sailed a war canoe to Minerva.)
For the ideological reasons mentioned above, there were no
weapons on Minerva to defend it.

Instead of forcefully defending or re-capturing Minerva, Oliver
put his faith in the World Court at the Hague or the UN, I've
forgotten which (both?).  In any event, he lost.  The reason
given?  Private citizens can not acquire sovereignty by any of
the recognized methods available to nations (i.e., discovery,
purchase or conquest).  At best, they can only make a claim on
behalf of an existing GOVERNMENT.  (Are we really surprised at
this outcome?)

Though Mike Oliver might disagree, I am confident that if he and
his associates had been willing to spill Tongan blood, their
claim would have been recognized.  It is much easier to get
forgiveness, than permission.

Dale also wrote:

    "Yes, it is *rare* for government intervention to
    improve the overall growth rate, but sometime it does.
    But I've never seen a libertarian admit that."

Depends on what you mean by "overall."  For the past quarter
century Singapore has had an impressive growth rate.  The credit
is usually given to the policies of Lee Kwan Yew--Singapore's
benevolent dictator (now semi-retired).  But if one looks at his
policies, the "government intervention" was mostly in the area of
personal freedom, NOT economics.  Though far from laissez-faire,
Singapore is much freer--economically and otherwise--than its
statist neighbors.  To the extent that the economy has been
"guided" or centrally planned, Singapore is headed for trouble.
So if "overall" means a couple of decades or so, government
intervention has seemed to improve things.  But if you take a
longer view, government intervention always "eats its young."

Can anyone think of an example where government intervention has
improved things on sum?  I can't.

