Subject: Econ: Forced economic growth

It seems clear that "improvements" to the infrastructure in Iraq since
the end of the UN war against Iraq have been impressive. I would think
these "imporvements" could be considered, "forced economic growth."

/Harry

Even Soviet "gains" in space technology, fighter jet technology, and
other "areas" have been forced and the infrastructure needed to build
jet fighters is rather impressive. THese things could be, again, called
forced economic growth; the question is at what cost.

Clearly the Japanesse govt., Al Gore, and may others see that there
is a "safe" or "good" or even "moral" need to have some "force" for
the good of society.

I want a "good society." I want to help people; I want people to have
a good chance of bettering themselves, of getting help when they need
it, etc. The difference is that I see that the best way to help them
(and myself) is without force! That the ends (read: use of force) never
justifies the means (read: the good of society) in such areas of economics
growth as we discuss here.

/Harry

