Date: Wed, 27 Jan 93 15:50:08 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: the free market

Trying to reply to various related messages in one batch:

   From: Mark Read Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>

   This must be your lucky day.  I, a hardcore, ideological Libertarian agree
   that State intervention could improve the overall growth rate of an economy.
   This involves distorting the economy in such a manner that resources are
   directed away from where people (if left unmolested) would have placed them
   and redirecting them into capital goods.  

   How about if someone is forced to live in a barracks with twenty other
   people, eat in a cafeteria, work eighteen hours a day, seven days a week and
   buy virtually nothing?   The money he earns is invested.  Five years later
   he's kicked out of the commune with enough resources to retire in comfort
   for life.  

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   Can you give me an instance, one, anywhere, any time, in which government
   regulations increased overall economic growth?

There is considerable evidence that Japan and South Korea have fueled
their growth partially by such a manipulation -- keeping consumption
lower (and investment higher) than a free market would give.  It
appears that the governments have a deliberate policy of keeping the
prices of consumption items high.  This tends to reduce consumption,
of course.  It looks like the children of the current generation, who
were raised in prosperity, are getting annoyed at the system, and are
demanding freer markets in consumption goods, i.e., increased levels
of consumption.

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   Can you actually give me
   a real instance in which government has improved a situation? I don't count,
   by the way, the government doing something like building a roadway or
   other actions that the private sector could have done.

Well, mass education is something that libertarians claim that the
private sector could do, but I've never seen the private sector *do*
it, and I've never seen people of any non-libertarian political
persuasion argue that the private sector could do it.

Another example, I've never heard of the ordinary streets of a nation
being paved by the private sector.  (Although the ability to build
computer-based toll systems might change that in the future.)

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   Yes, libertarians always denegrate government intervention, but you are
   completely mixing up cause and effect. We don't see any evidence that
   government intervention has ever caused any good and see lots that it
   has consistantly caused harm, so we fight it. That does not mean our
   standard of the good is "less government". In the arguments I always see
   on the net, things generally come down to people saying things like
   "well, Hong Kong has low government interference and a high growth rate
   as a result", not "we like Hong Kong just because it has a low level
   of government interference."

   From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu

   Nevertheless, your original point was that libertarians determined the
   "goodness" or "quality" of an economy by measuring the amount of
   government intervention which is nonsense.  Most libertarians won't
   admit that some government intrusions are good. However, this is unrelated
   to your original argument.  Most libertarians determine the goodness of
   economy by the growth rate and standard of living, hence Hong Kong
   is a good economy because it has a very high growth rate and modest
   standard of living.

Have you ever argued with an anti-nuke?  They all say that they are
against nuclear power because it is unsafe.  But any attempt to
convince them that nuclear power is safe, or might one day possibly be
safe, is countered by a volley of arguments.  And many of these
arguments are not agreed with by anybody who is not a passionate
anti-nuke.  After a while, you get the idea that anti-nukes are really
against nuclear power per se, and the ranting about safety is a
(possibly subconscious) justification for it.

Similarly, all libertarians argue that non-intervention is good
because it maximizes growth rates.  But the arguments that
non-intervention always maximizes growth rates are a bit dubious.
Economics is like that, of course.  But more interestingly, I've never
heard a non-libertarian claim that non-intervention always maximizes
growth rates, even non-libertarians whose causes would not lose
anything by making that admission.  And many of the supporting
arguments seem to fly in the face of common sense -- not that that
means they are wrong, but that they propose solutions to certain
problems that seem to never have been executed successfully in the
real world.

In sum, I'm beginning to suspect that libertarians are more driven by
a passionate desire to minimize government intervention than they are
by a passionate desire to maximize economic growth.  And there seem to
be other hints of this -- for instance, our discussions of
intellectual property law revolve around what is "right", or how to
organize intellectual property law without government.  I do not
remember a reply that starts with "What regime of intellectual
property law maximizes economic growth?"

If you want an example of what I'm feeling, read some of RMS's
diatribes -- he proposes a thousand reasons why his doctrines would
not be fatal to the software industry.  And I can't directly refute
any of them.  But each of them seems to run counter to what is known
of the history and development of the software industry.  In the end,
you wind up suspecting that he is *not* motivated by an interest in
the software industry, but by an interest in destroying the concept of
intellectual property.

   > Yes, it is *rare* for government intervention to improve the overall
   > growth rate, but sometime it does.  But I've never seen a libertarian
   > admit that.

    Possibly because they fuck it up more often than they fix it?

Perhaps, but I've never seen that proved.

Dale

